\\varepsilon_{\\gamma,p}\\approx 4{\\cal D}kT^{\\prime}=4{\\cal D}k\\left(\\frac{E^{ \\prime}_{\\rm int}}{aV^{\\prime}}\\right)^{1/4},  where k is the Boltzmann constant and a the radiation constant. As shown in the bottom panel of Fig. 1, the blackbody peak of the photosphere emission mostly falls into the \\sim 15-50 eV energy band for the adopted parameters. For an observational frequency \\nu, the luminosity light curve can be calculated as

\\nu L_{\\nu}=\\frac{1}{\\max(\\tau,1)}\\frac{8\\pi^{2}{\\cal D}^{2}R^{2}}{h^{3}c^{2} }\\frac{(h\\nu/{\\cal D})^{4}}{\\exp(h\\nu/{\\cal D}kT^{\\prime})-1},  where h is the Planck constant. The light curves at different frequencies (1 eV, 30 eV, 1 keV) of the millisecond-magnetar-powered merger-novae are presented in Fig. 2. For nominal parameters, the emission mainly occurs in the UV band with a peak luminosity around 10^{45}erg s{}^{-1}. Higher-energy (e.g. X-ray) emission peaks earlier and the

